/*!
    \page creator-deployment-harmonyos.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-harmonyos

    \title Specify HarmonyOS deploy settings

    A HarmonyOS device runs an application from a signed HarmonyOS Ability
    Package (\c {.hap}), so deploying means building one. Go to
    \uicontrol Projects > \uicontrol {Deploy Settings} to see the steps of
    \uicontrol {Deploy to HarmonyOS device}:

    \list 1
        \li \uicontrol {Build HarmonyOS package (.hap)} collects what the
            application needs. It runs \c harmonydeployqt to stage the Qt
            libraries, the QML imports and the plugins, adds the libraries the
            staged ones need in turn, and generates the hvigor project.
        \li \uicontrol {Package HarmonyOS application with hvigor} turns that
            project into an unsigned package.
        \li \uicontrol {Sign HarmonyOS package} signs it with
            \c hap-sign-tool.jar and the material from
            \preferences > \uicontrol SDKs > \uicontrol HarmonyOS.
        \li \uicontrol {Install HarmonyOS package with hdc} installs it on the
            device.
    \endlist

    Turn off \uicontrol {Run without installing} in
    \preferences > \uicontrol SDKs > \uicontrol HarmonyOS to always reinstall,
    which is what a changed package needs.

    \section1 Libraries the device does not have

    Qt links against ICU, \c fontconfig, \c freetype, and \c libpng, but a
    HarmonyOS device carries none of them. Set \uicontrol {Additional packages}
    to the prefix they are installed under, and the kit passes it to the build
    as \c QT_ADDITIONAL_PACKAGES_PREFIX_PATH, which adds them to the package.
    When one is missing anyway, \QC reports which staged library needs it.

    \section1 What the application is called on the device

    A provisioning profile is issued for one bundle name, and a device installs
    a package only under that name. \QC therefore uses the name from the
    profile rather than the one in the project, and reports it when the two
    differ.

    \sa {Build and Run}{How to: Build and Run},
    {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for HarmonyOS},
    {Developing for HarmonyOS devices}, {Sign HarmonyOS packages},
    {Run on HarmonyOS devices}
*/
